;;; regression: skip-under-interp
;;; The mapping functions and the sequence functions that build a result must
;;; stay within the size of that result (plus a capturing closure when the
;;; function argument captures a local). Allocation is asserted only on emitted
;;; code: the interpreter does not use the direct entries measured here.
;;;
;;; Each row is a named DEFUN so the compiler sees the callee. A capturing
;;; lambda is made once per row call, as it is in the probe, and its 248 bytes
;;; are part of the budget. Object sizes were measured with MAKE-ARRAY and
;;; MAKE-STRING: a cons is 32 bytes, an N-element vector 112 + 8N, and a
;;; fresh N-character string a LispString (32) plus a string (24 + 2N, rounded
;;; to 8 bytes), which measured 72 for 8 characters.

